q-70523/selection/5d121e8e91ff98432a5b53d7805976708cebc622aeffcbeeea76b2a57224e857" "q-70523/selection/176d6a1c48fc4e847de05425e2e71ac42b2c598bb7e981f223bbaf99dc8dbd4f" "q-70523/selection/6d549c6ce7ced9179dc8d49f7d02d75fdc23c4e9446fe069e8ca73a2429604d7" "q-70523/selection/c1921d27d54d33c0fe1cbeaeafc6b6387a0295efd4e1e1faa9fd4cd62fe56dde" "q-70523/selection/d855321e9e2aee56e15a02656f6576e93b5b72734dc6c6ebe8b7a807d91748cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70523" :question/id #uuid "2602fecb-27f9-522e-9ca3-fc225206d948" :question/math-academy-id "q-70523" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+5 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5\\le 1+5 \\\\ 4 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $4\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6-7 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7\\le 6-7 \\\\ -13 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-13\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $4\\le x\\le 6$ and $-13\\le y\\le -1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-70523/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70523"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-327866/field-1/3481