c-5317-8a06-708e7bb1dc72" :question/math-academy-id "q-327884" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{7}{3},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\le -\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{7}{3}\\le \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{29}{6} & \\le y\\le \\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{29}{6}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-327884/field-1" "q-327884/field-2" "q-327884/field-3" "q-327884/field-4" "q-327884/field-5" "q-327884/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-327884"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5075"] :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3,\n$$\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5098"] :question/problem "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n\n$$\nx=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038739/assets/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5099"] :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty , \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-6t \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left( t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0,$ we can conclude that $x \\geq -9.$\n\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4,$ notice that $t^4 \\geq 0,$ and consequently $y \\geq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x \\geq -9$ and $y \\geq 4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5292"] :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$ and $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
